.TH SH 1 "2026-07-27" "1.0" "Shell Manual"
.SH NAME
sh, app_gui.py, sh-gui \- Custom Python-based Unix Shell and Simple Desktop Terminal Emulator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sh
.br
.B python3 app_gui.py
.br
.B sh-gui
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sh
is a custom Unix command shell implemented in Python. It features an interactive Read-Eval-Print Loop (REPL), Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) parsing, pseudo-terminal allocation for interactive applications, pipeline chaining, stream redirection, environment variable expansion, persistent command history, background job tracking, and a native PyQt6 Desktop Terminal Application (\fBapp_gui.py\fR / \fBsh-gui\fR).
.SH DESKTOP TERMINAL APPLICATION (GUI)
The desktop application provides a simple graphical terminal interface with:
.TP
.B Multi-Tab Management
Bottom-positioned, left-aligned tab bar. Open new shell tabs with \fBCmd+T\fR (or \fBCtrl+Shift+T\fR) and close tabs with \fBCmd+W\fR (or \fBCtrl+W\fR).
.TP
.B Native Canvas Renderer
Custom 2D \fBQPainter\fR grid character renderer with floating-point sub-pixel advance alignment, isolated cursor blink repaints, drag-selection, copy/paste (\fBCmd+C\fR / \fBCmd+V\fR), and tab completion.
.TP
.B Font Zooming
Scale terminal text on the fly using \fBCmd+=\fR (Zoom In), \fBCmd+-\fR (Zoom Out), and \fBCmd+0\fR (Reset Zoom to default 21pt).
.TP
.B Log Export & Controls
Export terminal scrollback buffer logs directly to file via the \fBSave Log\fR toolbar action. Clear screen with \fBCtrl+L\fR.

.SH BUILTIN COMMANDS
.TP
.B cd [directory]
Change the current working directory to \fIdirectory\fR. If no argument is specified, defaults to the user's home directory (\fB~\fR).
.TP
.B pwd
Print the absolute path of the current working directory.
.TP
.B type \fR[\fIcommand\fR ...]
Indicate how each specified \fIcommand\fR would be interpreted (as a shell builtin or an executable file in \fB$PATH\fR).
.TP
.B echo \fR[\fIarg\fR ...]
Display arguments separated by single spaces followed by a newline.
.TP
.B jobs
List active background jobs managed by the shell and reap terminated background processes.
.TP
.B history
Display the numbered list of previous commands stored in readline history.
.TP
.B exit
Terminate the shell session.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES & EXPANSION
.TP
.B $?
Expands to the exit status code of the last executed command.
.TP
.B $VAR
Expands to the value of the environment variable \fIVAR\fR (e.g., \fB$USER\fR, \fB$PATH\fR).
.TP
.B ~
Expands to the user's home directory.
.SH REDIRECTION OPERATORS
The shell supports standard stream redirections for both external commands and builtins:
.TP
.B > \fR, \fB1>
Redirect standard output to a file (overwrites).
.TP
.B >> \fR, \fB1>>
Redirect standard output to a file (appends).
.TP
.B 2>
Redirect standard error to a file (overwrites).
.TP
.B 2>>
Redirect standard error to a file (appends).
.TP
.B 2>&1
Merge standard error into standard output.
.TP
.B &> \fR, \fB>&
Redirect both standard output and standard error to a file (overwrites).
.TP
.B &>>
Redirect both standard output and standard error to a file (appends).
.TP
.B <
Redirect standard input from a file.
.SH PIPELINES
Commands connected by a pipe character (\fB|\fR) form a pipeline where the standard output of the left-hand command is connected via a Unix pipe descriptor to the standard input of the right-hand command. Builtin commands fully participate in pipeline chains.
.SH JOB CONTROL
Append an ampersand (\fB&\fR) to the end of a command to run it asynchronously in the background. The shell prints the assigned job ID and PID, then immediately returns control to the REPL prompt. Use the \fBjobs\fR builtin to check status.
.SH PROMPT & HISTORY
The shell prompt displays the basename of the current working directory and the exit code of the last command in \fBGREEN\fR (exit code 0) or \fBRED\fR (non-zero). Command history is automatically saved to \fB~/.mysh_history\fR upon exit and restored on launch.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a command in the background:
.PP
.RS
sleep 10 &
.RE
.PP
Filter command output through a pipeline to a file:
.PP
.RS
history | grep cd > output.txt
.RE
.PP
Expand environment variables and exit codes:
.PP
.RS
echo Current user is $USER with last status $?
.RE